WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.168
GRADY POWELL: Eric, Dave.

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:03.403
Congratulations, gentlemen.

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:04.904
You guys have solidified
yourselves spots

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:06.806
in our third and final
round of this competition.

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:09.042
Now, in this final round, we're
gonna be sending you guys back

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:12.479
to your home forges to build
yet another iconic weapon

00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:13.980
from Korean history.

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:15.382
We're gonna have
you build this--

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:21.387
the woldo.

00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:23.088
DOUG MARCAIDA: The woldo
is a pole-arm weapon

00:00:23.155 --> 00:00:25.090
that has been wielded
by Korean warriors

00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:26.926
since the sixth century.

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:29.228
The large crescent
blade provided the user

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:32.298
with incredible force to deliver
lethal blows and deep cuts

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:34.400
with one swing into an opponent.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.503
Historically, this heavy
weapon required special skills

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.807
and was only wielded in battle
by elite Korean warriors.

00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:44.510
Today, Korean martial artists
who specialize in the woldo

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.913
perform intricate maneuvers
with this intimidating weapon at

00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:50.483
festivals and cultural events.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.851
All right, gentlemen.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.486
We're giving you four days
to build your woldo swords.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.755
And I want to see your best
work, because one of them

00:00:56.822 --> 00:00:58.857
will be worth $10,000.

00:00:58.924 --> 00:00:59.859
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:02.062 line:14%
[music playing]

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:07.233 line:8%
ERIC HEMKER: My name is Eric
Hemker, I'm 29 years old,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.735 line:14%
and I'm a design engineer.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:11.137
I design seat
structures for cars.

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:14.406
And it's very important,
because if that person's not

00:01:14.506 --> 00:01:17.577
comfortable, they're
not gonna buy that car.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.011
Same thing goes for a knife.

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:20.613
If that is not
comfortable, they're

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.315
not gonna wield that knife.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:23.616
There we go.

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.185
The woldo is in good shape.

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:27.787
I've got plenty of meat on there
that I can grind back and get

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:28.888
to the shape I'm looking for.

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:30.857
[music playing]

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:36.529
That is a hard blade.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.330
Overall, good clinch.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.200
So I decided, as a little
decorative embellishment,

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.135
to add a little
grip to the handle.

00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:46.339 line:8%
I'm gonna flute and
inlay some paracord.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:50.876
I'm using imperial red
and royal blue to honor

00:01:50.976 --> 00:01:52.945
the colors of the Korean flag.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:53.780
So I'm pleased.

00:01:57.884 --> 00:01:58.951
DAVE ARMOUR: My
name is Dave Armour

00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:00.386
and I am from Auburn, Illinois.

00:02:00.452 --> 00:02:02.588 line:8%
I got into bladesmithing
when I was 15.

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:06.059 line:8%
I went to a theme park where
they had a gun and knife shop,

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:08.995
and a man was demonstrating
how to make a knife.

00:02:09.061 --> 00:02:10.496
Being 15 and just
being fascinated,

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:12.765
I followed him into his shop
and asked so many questions,

00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:15.535
he literally took a book on
knife making that was for sale,

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:17.837
threw it at me, and said,
get the hell out of the shop.

00:02:17.903 --> 00:02:19.939
I may have ruined this early on.

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.573
I forged a piece over
so that, actually,

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.243
the top part was actually
folding over on itself.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.577 line:14%
It's called a cold shut.

00:02:25.644 --> 00:02:27.046 line:8%
Causes all kinds of
problems later on, 'cause

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:28.648 line:14%
the pieces aren't attached.

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:30.482
I go over to the grinder.

00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:32.985
If I can grind out,
I'm not too bad.

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:34.253
[exhales]

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:36.622
So right now, it's just
back to the grinder.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:38.357
Good news is,
everything's solid.

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:40.626
The cold shut I was worried
about is grinding out.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.362
I have a blade
ready to heat treat.

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:45.698
Hup.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.071
I'm straight.

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:53.773
[scraping]

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:54.840
Yup.

00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:56.241
And it's hard.

00:02:56.341 --> 00:02:57.776
That's as good as I
can hope for right now.

00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:01.013
So I grabbed some pink paracord
that I'm gonna use to wrap

00:03:01.079 --> 00:03:02.347
the areas between the collars.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.717
And then I have a pink
wood stain that I'm going

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:06.285
to stay in the hickory with.

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:08.621
When I do a knife show, I always
have one knife on my table

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:09.555
that has a pink handle.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:11.291
It's not the
typical-looking thing,

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.792
but I think it looks kind of
neat so I'm gonna go with it.

00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:15.395
I think this is a solid piece.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:18.731
I think it'll test well, but
we're gonna see what happens.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:20.466
[music playing]

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.035 line:14%
DOUG MARCAIDA: Bladesmiths--

00:03:23.101 --> 00:03:26.439
--and welcome to the KEAL test.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:28.607
To find out what kind of lethal
damage your weapons will do,

00:03:28.674 --> 00:03:30.976
I will take your weapons
and deliver lethal blows

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:32.511
on this ballistics dummy.

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:34.046
Eric, you're first.
You ready for this?

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:34.880
- Yes, sir.
- Right.

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:35.748
Let's do this.

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:37.684
[music playing]

00:03:43.055 --> 00:03:49.629
[splatting]

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:01.974
All right, Eric.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.977
Let's talk about
your woldo here.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:05.477
It is forward-heavy.

00:04:05.577 --> 00:04:08.314
There's a lot of metal in
here, but it is wieldable.

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:10.850
Now, your edge is sharp.

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:13.452
It penetrates deeply into
this ballistics dummy,

00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:15.721
cutting the gel torso
and into the bones.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:19.892
Over all, sir, your
woldo, it will KEAL.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:21.026
 Thank you.

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:21.961
 All right, Dave.
Your turn, sir.

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:22.695
You ready?

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:23.195
 Yes, sir.

00:04:23.296 --> 00:04:24.063
 Let's do this.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.266
[music playing]

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:34.941
[splatting]

00:04:46.051 --> 00:04:46.718
[thump]

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:48.821
[laughs]

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:50.656
DOUG MARCAIDA: All right, Dave.

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.191
Let's talk about
your woldo here.

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:54.426
It is a little bit heavier
than the other weapon.

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:57.029
Heavier weapons require
a lot of adjustments.

00:04:57.096 --> 00:05:00.399
But your edge here is sharp
with this forward weight

00:05:00.465 --> 00:05:03.302
that cuts deep into
this ballistics dummy

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:05.271
and cuts the bones inside.

00:05:05.337 --> 00:05:07.974
But in doing so, sir,
your edge over here

00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:10.009
did take a little
bit of rolling.

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:13.612
But the other parts are fine.

00:05:13.712 --> 00:05:17.716
And overall, sir,
your woldo will KEAL.

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:18.551
 Thank you.

00:05:24.057 --> 00:05:25.057
DAVE BAKER: All
right, gentlemen.

00:05:25.157 --> 00:05:26.158
You know what time it is.

00:05:26.224 --> 00:05:27.459
It's time for the strength test.

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:30.796
We're calling this one
the Bamboo Man Attack.

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:33.432
To test the overall
construction of your blade,

00:05:33.498 --> 00:05:35.968
I'm going to take them
and gently tap them

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:37.236
against our bamboo
men over here.

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:38.771
[laughter]

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:41.006
Now, remember, this is not
about what your weapons

00:05:41.073 --> 00:05:42.441
do to these targets.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:43.642
It's about what these
targets do to your weapons.

00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:44.977
Eric, you're up first.
You ready?

00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:45.744
 Yes, sir.

00:05:45.811 --> 00:05:48.013
 All right.

00:05:48.113 --> 00:05:48.781
[grunts]

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:50.750
[music playing]

00:05:51.283 --> 00:05:56.489
[grunting]

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:08.468
 [laughs]

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:13.372
DAVE BAKER: So Eric, right
off, this is a heavy beast.

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:14.673
Your balance point's not bad.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.209
I kind of like that
right there where

00:06:17.275 --> 00:06:18.677
you can manipulate the weapon.

00:06:18.744 --> 00:06:21.514
As far as the weapon
performance goes,

00:06:21.581 --> 00:06:23.382
you maintain an edge
all the way along.

00:06:23.448 --> 00:06:24.650
It's still sharp.

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:25.317
Nicely done.

00:06:25.384 --> 00:06:26.652
 Thank you.

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:27.920
 All right, Dave.
You're up.

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:28.921
Ready?
- Yes, sir.

00:06:28.988 --> 00:06:30.923
[music playing]

00:06:35.861 --> 00:06:39.799
 [grunting]

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:57.550
All right, Dave.

00:06:57.616 --> 00:07:00.719
Your weapon is
really, really heavy.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:04.222
So you can make a
devastating cut,

00:07:04.289 --> 00:07:06.926
but recovery is really slow.

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:12.531
As far as the blade, the blade
itself is dented sideways

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:15.434
here and up above here.

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:19.971
But still solid, so you
survived quite nicely.

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:20.839
Well done, Dave.

00:07:20.906 --> 00:07:22.374
 Thank you.

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:24.510
[music playing]

00:07:25.744 --> 00:07:27.079
DOUG MARCAIDA: Bladesmiths,
this is the sharpness

00:07:27.179 --> 00:07:30.249
test, the Sugar Cane Slice.

00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:31.683
To find out how sharp
your weapons are,

00:07:31.783 --> 00:07:34.019
I'm gonna attempt to cut
through all these sugar canes.

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:36.622
Unlike the strength test,
this is all about what

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:40.026
your weapons do and how well
they cut the sugar canes.

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:41.761
Eric, are you ready to do this?

00:07:41.861 --> 00:07:42.294
 Yes, sir.

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:44.597
[music playing]

00:07:52.838 --> 00:07:54.474
[crash]

00:07:55.841 --> 00:07:57.477
[thumps]

00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:03.049
 All right, Eric.

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:05.017
Let's talk about your edge here.

00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:06.685
Your edge, it's sharp.

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.287
It cut through all
the sugar canes.

00:08:08.387 --> 00:08:11.123
And overall, sir, your
weapon, it will cut.

00:08:11.223 --> 00:08:12.257
 Thank you.

00:08:12.357 --> 00:08:13.191
 Dave, it's your turn, sir.

00:08:13.258 --> 00:08:14.060
Are you ready for this?

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:14.760
 Yes, sir.

00:08:14.861 --> 00:08:16.729
[music playing]

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:30.009
[crash]

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:31.978
(QUIETLY) Damn.

00:08:40.218 --> 00:08:41.320
 All right, Dave.

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:42.988
Let's talk about
your weapon here.

00:08:43.055 --> 00:08:46.892
It's still sharp and it cuts
cleanly all the way through.

00:08:46.992 --> 00:08:50.295
But the weight of your blade,
as I'm cutting, dragged downward

00:08:50.362 --> 00:08:51.730
and I ran out of sugar cane.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.899
But overall, sir, your
weapon, it will cut.

00:08:53.999 --> 00:08:54.800
 Thank you.

00:08:57.536 --> 00:08:59.038
GRADY POWELL: Well,
gentlemen, you

00:08:59.138 --> 00:09:01.640
both performed extremely well
in our Korean theme challenge.

00:09:01.706 --> 00:09:04.610
Both your woldos did
great during our tests.

00:09:04.677 --> 00:09:07.513
But this is a competition
and there has to be a winner.

00:09:07.579 --> 00:09:09.815
The judges have made
the decision and today's

00:09:09.915 --> 00:09:11.551
"Forged in Fire" champion is--

00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:16.388
Eric.

00:09:16.455 --> 00:09:18.090
Congratulations.

00:09:18.157 --> 00:09:19.525
Now, Dave, you fought hard.

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:21.293
But unfortunately, you're
not today's winner,

00:09:21.393 --> 00:09:23.696
and Dave is gonna tell you why.

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:25.530
DAVE BAKER: Dave, your blade
did great in the KEAL test

00:09:25.597 --> 00:09:27.466
and did well in
the sharpness test.

00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:29.602
What this came down
to are two things.

00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:31.703
The overall weight of
your blade and the damage

00:09:31.803 --> 00:09:33.071
it took in the strength test.

00:09:33.138 --> 00:09:34.506
Those are the reasons
we're letting you go.

00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:35.507
 I understand.

00:09:35.574 --> 00:09:36.875
Thanks for having me.

00:09:36.942 --> 00:09:38.043
GRADY POWELL: Well,
Dave, it's clear you have

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:39.378
what it takes to
fight in this forge.

00:09:39.478 --> 00:09:40.912
You're an extremely
talented smith.

00:09:41.012 --> 00:09:42.982
But unfortunately, your time
in this competition has ended.

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:45.051
And for that reason, I'm gonna
have to ask you to please

00:09:45.151 --> 00:09:46.518
step off the forge floor.

00:09:46.585 --> 00:09:48.620
 (WHISPERING) Good job, man.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:49.955
 Well, Eric, you
know what that means.

00:09:50.055 --> 00:09:51.691
You are the newest
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:51.791 --> 00:09:54.259
You're gonna be walking out of
here with a check for $10,000.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:54.994
Congratulations.

00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:56.262
[applause]

00:09:57.363 --> 00:09:58.830
ERIC HEMKER: I feel
awesome, coming

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:01.233
out here, winning the
competition, having my skills

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:02.501
validated.

00:10:02.568 --> 00:10:04.636
This is a crowning
achievement of mine.

00:10:04.703 --> 00:10:05.403
 Well done.

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:06.605
 Thank you.

00:10:06.705 --> 00:10:08.807
If somebody wants
me to make a woldo,

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:11.978
it's gonna cost 'em $10,000.

